orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1842:   243	Write four skill documents under `skills/` (not installed anywhere by the build): `planning-worker.md` (how a dispatched agent works a ticket via the CLI: orient with `plan ticket show --json`, propose via stdin, recap discipline, heartbeats, never asking questions), `planning-boundary.md` (the judgment pass contract), `planner-main.md` (the day/system chat agent: operating the queues and day plan via CLI, and quick capture — anything the human tosses into the day chat that is work or an idea gets filed as a standalone ticket or idea via the CLI, immediately, defaulting to P3), `planning-executor.md` (working an in_progress ticket you own, including the review discipline: at needs_review, orchestrate the Codex CLI against the result and the review notes, fix what it surfaces, update the notes). Each ≤ 150 lines, written for the agent that will load it. DOCS.md summarizes their roles.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1848:   249	**Build in this order**: (1) contracts skeleton — schema DDL, enums, typed models, adapter interfaces, config loading, CLI/API surface stubs; (2) **the verify instrument** (18.2), runnable with every checklist item FAIL; (3) pure-logic modules + unit tests (items 1–21 and 36) green; (4) server wiring — API, WS, resolution engine over DB, dispatcher, boundary scheduler, CLI against the API; (5) UI views; (6) Playwright e2e suite, items 22–34 green; (7) the fake-ticket dogfood pass (Section 18.5): Level A green (item 35), Levels B and C executed with evidence recorded in DOGFOOD.md. Do not start a later stage while an earlier stage's tests fail.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1856:   257	- **DOCS.md** — plain-language documentation written progressively as each stage completes, never retrofitted. A smart non-engineer must be able to read it: what the planner is, the entities, how proposals and ceilings work, what the dispatcher does, how a day flows, what each screen shows, what the CLI verbs do, what the skills are for. If a section needs the code to be understood, rewrite the section.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1864:   265	- Unit items are 1–21 and 36; end-to-end items are 22–35. Item 34 (snapshot migration) runs against `migration/source-snapshot/`, and item 35 (dogfood Level A) is the scripted walkthrough — both part of the suite, not manual steps. Levels B and C of the dogfood (Section 18.5) are deliberately OUTSIDE `./verify`: they use live agents and belong to stage 7's evidence, not the deterministic gate.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1885:   286	9. Blocking: ticket blocked by an open ticket is dispatch-ineligible; blocker → `done` makes it eligible; `blocks` cycle creation rejected.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1890:   291	14. Claim CAS: two concurrent claim attempts on one ticket → exactly one wins, one `runs` row created.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1892:   293	16. Circuit breaker: two consecutive `crashed` runs → `auto_blocked`, ineligible; unblock action clears flag and counter; a `done` run resets the counter.
