orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6623:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:581:249:**Build in this order**: (1) contracts skeleton — schema DDL, enums, typed models, adapter interfaces, config loading, CLI/API surface stubs; (2) **the verify instrument** (18.2), runnable with every checklist item FAIL; (3) pure-logic modules + unit tests (items 1–21 and 36) green; (4) server wiring — API, WS, resolution engine over DB, dispatcher, boundary scheduler, CLI against the API; (5) UI views; (6) Playwright e2e suite, items 22–34 green; (7) the fake-ticket dogfood pass (Section 18.5): Level A green (item 35), Levels B and C executed with evidence recorded in DOGFOOD.md. Do not start a later stage while an earlier stage's tests fail.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6625:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:583:265:- Unit items are 1–21 and 36; end-to-end items are 22–35. Item 34 (snapshot migration) runs against `migration/source-snapshot/`, and item 35 (dogfood Level A) is the scripted walkthrough — both part of the suite, not manual steps. Levels B and C of the dogfood (Section 18.5) are deliberately OUTSIDE `./verify`: they use live agents and belong to stage 7's evidence, not the deterministic gate.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6626:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:586:314:35. Dogfood Level A: `scripts/dogfood_cli.py` drives the full workflow against a test server seeded with `plan seed --demo` — agent actions through the real CLI, human actions (accepts with grant pairs, needs_review approve, day-plan decisions) through the HTTP API, since the CLI deliberately has no human verbs: create a ticket; propose and accept through every gate — every accept carrying its grant pair — including one edit-accept and one superseded proposal; write and overwrite the recap; assign to the day and remove; create a blocking link and confirm dispatch-ineligibility, then complete the blocker and confirm eligibility; claim via the test dispatcher tick; propose result with claim env; approve from needs_review to done. The script asserts each step's resulting state via `--json`/response output and exits non-zero on the first mismatch.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6628:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:699:   159	Through a **spawn adapter** (subprocess boundary, mockable): `hermes -p <profile> --skills <skill> chat -q "work planning ticket <id>"`, detached session, per-run log file under `data/logs/`, env: `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID`, `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`. Profile and skill names come from config [RULING R3 default: profile `default`, skill `planning-worker`]. Tests never spawn real processes.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6629:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:707:   167	Every agent write (proposal, recap, heartbeat, run close) must carry `PLAN_RUN_ID` + `PLAN_CLAIM` matching the ticket's active claim, except proposal/recap writes from non-dispatched agent contexts (no claim env — e.g. the planner-main chat agent), which are permitted with the proposer taken from `PLAN_ACTOR` (default `agent`). Human writes happen through the UI/API only — the CLI is an agent tool. A write with a stale/foreign claim is rejected with a structured error naming the mismatch.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6630:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1842:   243	Write four skill documents under `skills/` (not installed anywhere by the build): `planning-worker.md` (how a dispatched agent works a ticket via the CLI: orient with `plan ticket show --json`, propose via stdin, recap discipline, heartbeats, never asking questions), `planning-boundary.md` (the judgment pass contract), `planner-main.md` (the day/system chat agent: operating the queues and day plan via CLI, and quick capture — anything the human tosses into the day chat that is work or an idea gets filed as a standalone ticket or idea via the CLI, immediately, defaulting to P3), `planning-executor.md` (working an in_progress ticket you own, including the review discipline: at needs_review, orchestrate the Codex CLI against the result and the review notes, fix what it surfaces, update the notes). Each ≤ 150 lines, written for the agent that will load it. DOCS.md summarizes their roles.
orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6631:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:1848:   249	**Build in this order**: (1) contracts skeleton — schema DDL, enums, typed models, adapter interfaces, config loading, CLI/API surface stubs; (2) **the verify instrument** (18.2), runnable with every checklist item FAIL; (3) pure-logic modules + unit tests (items 1–21 and 36) green; (4) server wiring — API, WS, resolution engine over DB, dispatcher, boundary scheduler, CLI against the API; (5) UI views; (6) Playwright e2e suite, items 22–34 green; (7) the fake-ticket dogfood pass (Section 18.5): Level A green (item 35), Levels B and C executed with evidence recorded in DOGFOOD.md. Do not start a later stage while an earlier stage's tests fail.
