orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5018:./DOCS.md:7:The planner is a personal planning system that runs entirely on one computer. It replaces a folder of markdown files with a small database and a web page. It keeps track of four kinds of things:
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5021:./DOCS.md:39:- **Stage 3 — the rules (done):** all of the system's judgment now exists as tested code, before any server or screen. The resolution engine enforces everything described above: one pending proposal per blank (a newer one replaces the older, and the replacement is recorded); automatic acceptance strictly below the ceiling; the mandatory "how far next" answer on every human approval; the special ending where finished work goes to review unless the human had already granted straight-to-done. The day machinery knows what "today" means (the day flips at 5am, not midnight) and how the morning handover works: it closes yesterday with a count of what got done, carries forward what didn't, and asks the brief-writer for a plan — skipping that entirely if the human already planned the day. The dispatcher's bookkeeping is in place: an agent claims a ticket by winning a single atomic database update (two agents can race; exactly one wins), holds it on a 15-minute lease it must keep renewing, and loses it if it goes quiet or dies. Two failures in a row on the same ticket trip a breaker that parks it until the human clears it. And the importer reads the old markdown planning folder into the database — proven against a frozen copy of the real planning data, item for item, with an explicit list of everything it chose not to import (nothing is ever dropped silently), and safe to run twice (the second run imports nothing new). The scoreboard stands at 22 of 36.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5022:./DOCS.md:40:- **Stage 4 — the server (done):** the rules now answer the door. One local process serves a JSON interface for every action in the system, and it enforces who may do what: requests from a working agent must carry a valid claim on the ticket they're touching (a stale or borrowed claim is refused, and the refusal names what was wrong without ever revealing the live claim itself); the actions reserved for the human — approving, granting, unblocking, dropping, freezing, deciding the day plan — refuse agents entirely. A live feed announces every change so screens can refresh themselves; the feed is only a doorbell, never the data. The dispatcher and the morning boundary job now actually run on their schedules (in testing they run only when explicitly ticked, so tests are exact). The command-line tool agents use is complete: every verb talks to the server, returns clean machine-readable output, and deliberately contains no approval powers at all. Four review findings worth naming, all caught by the independent reviewer and fixed: a path that leaked the live claim token into an error detail; a check that ran validation before verifying the claim; a number that could sneak into a date field untyped; and an environment value that could trick the tool into reading the wrong input.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5023:./DOCS.md:41:- **Stage 5 — the screens (done):** six screens on one small design system whose entire personality lives in a single token file. Day: the morning brief, the proposed plan with accept/invalidate per line, the day's tickets, and the day's chat. Review: the one-at-a-time approval walk — and the approve button physically refuses to work until "how far may the agent go next" has been answered, both halves. Board: every ticket by stage. Ticket: the whole story of one piece of work — the four blanks, the permission dials, links, runs, history, chat, and a copy button that produces a plain-text block for pasting anywhere. Sprint: the kickoff (locked once frozen), the items with progress rollups, and the review. Backlog & Ideas: what's waiting, and what's merely interesting. The reviewer's best catch here was in the markdown renderer: a crafted link that browsers would quietly treat as runnable code; it's now impossible to express.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5025:./DOCS.md:51:The `plan` tool is for agents; every verb talks to the server and answers in machine-readable JSON. `ticket create/show/list/set` manage tickets (set covers only priority, deadline, day and sprint placement — permissions are never settable here). `propose <field>` files a proposal with the text piped in; `recap` writes the running summary; `note <field>` leaves guidance on a blank without touching it. `item`, `sprint show`, `idea`, `day`, and `link` do what they say. `run heartbeat/close` are how a dispatched agent keeps its claim alive and reports how its run ended. `queue approvals/pickup/overdue` are read-only views of what's waiting. There is deliberately no verb for accepting, approving, granting, or unblocking anything — those live only in the human's web interface. `plan serve` runs the server; `plan seed` imports the old markdown system or creates the demo dataset.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5026:./DOCS.md:55:Four short documents under `skills/` teach specific agents their jobs: **planning-worker** — how a dispatched agent works a ticket through the CLI (orient, propose, recap, heartbeat, never ask questions); **planning-boundary** — the contract for writing the morning brief and plan proposal; **planner-main** — how the day-chat agent operates the queues through the CLI, including its standing instruction to file quick captures (work or ideas mentioned in chat) immediately as tickets or ideas via the CLI; **planning-executor** — how an agent executing an in-progress ticket works, including the review discipline at the checking stage: run the independent reviewer against the result and the review notes, fix what it finds, update the notes, and leave the final say to the human.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5059:./config.yaml:15:worker_skill: planning-worker      # dispatched-agent skill (R3)
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5083:./decisions.md:112:Fallback-audit concerns, dispositioned: (1) Body→success.notes remains the only §4.2-legal home, per D13 — refuted as a violation, acknowledged as the documented spec tension. (2) Level C's wrapper shim is disclosed in DOGFOOD.md; §18.5 sanctions materially different prompt shapes across attempts, and installing skills into ~/.hermes is explicitly post-run (§18.4) — the stock command's behavior (attempt 1) is honestly recorded as the failure that motivated the shim. (3) Level B's worker overreach is disclosed verbatim in DOGFOOD.md; the mechanical evidence stands. (4) pyproject declares, requirements.txt pins — the spec's wording is satisfied; the venv is built from the pins. (5) The auditor cannot run ./verify in its sandbox — the goal's final demonstration runs it fresh alongside the audit in the same transcript.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:5084:./decisions.md:128:1. **§7.1/§7.3 dead-worker detection.** `RealSpawnAdapter.spawn()` discarded the `Popen` handle, so the server never reaped its own children; `_pid_alive` used `os.kill(pid,0)`, which reports a zombie (exited-but-unreaped child) as alive — so the tick's required "detect dead worker processes" never fired for spawned workers until the 15-minute TTL. Accepted. Fix (Fix A): the real spawn adapter now retains child handles and exposes a liveness method that reaps via `Popen.poll()` — an exited child is detected and reclaimed within one tick; untracked pids (e.g. a claim surviving a server restart) fall back to the old `os.kill(pid,0)` probe. Test-mode liveness (`_pid_alive_always`) is unchanged, so verify is unaffected. Reaping logic is unit-tested with an injected Popen stub (no real process spawned, per §7.4/§14). DOGFOOD systemic-finding #1 updated from "present" to "fixed"; finding #2 (reclaim leaving the breaker unchanged) is spec-correct per §7.5 and left as a noted observation.
