orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:8917:DOCS.md:40:- **Stage 4 — the server (done):** the rules now answer the door. One local process serves a JSON interface for every action in the system, and it enforces who may do what: requests from a working agent must carry a valid claim on the ticket they're touching (a stale or borrowed claim is refused, and the refusal names what was wrong without ever revealing the live claim itself); the actions reserved for the human — approving, granting, unblocking, dropping, freezing, deciding the day plan — refuse agents entirely. A live feed announces every change so screens can refresh themselves; the feed is only a doorbell, never the data. The dispatcher and the morning boundary job now actually run on their schedules (in testing they run only when explicitly ticked, so tests are exact). The command-line tool agents use is complete: every verb talks to the server, returns clean machine-readable output, and deliberately contains no approval powers at all. Four review findings worth naming, all caught by the independent reviewer and fixed: a path that leaked the live claim token into an error detail; a check that ran validation before verifying the claim; a number that could sneak into a date field untyped; and an environment value that could trick the tool into reading the wrong input.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:8918:DOCS.md:41:- **Stage 5 — the screens (done):** six screens on one small design system whose entire personality lives in a single token file. Day: the morning brief, the proposed plan with accept/invalidate per line, the day's tickets, and the day's chat. Review: the one-at-a-time approval walk — and the approve button physically refuses to work until "how far may the agent go next" has been answered, both halves. Board: every ticket by stage. Ticket: the whole story of one piece of work — the four blanks, the permission dials, links, runs, history, chat, and a copy button that produces a plain-text block for pasting anywhere. Sprint: the kickoff (locked once frozen), the items with progress rollups, and the review. Backlog & Ideas: what's waiting, and what's merely interesting. The reviewer's best catch here was in the markdown renderer: a crafted link that browsers would quietly treat as runnable code; it's now impossible to express.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:8919:DOCS.md:42:- **Stage 6 — the browser proof (done):** thirteen browser tests drive the real thing end to end — a real server, a real database file, real Chromium, and the real command-line tool running as a subprocess, with two independent browser windows open where the test demands proof that a change made in one place appears in the other without anyone refreshing. They cover: creating work from the command line and watching it appear on the Board; a proposal surviving the trip through the approval screen with the mandatory "how far next" answer; the morning boundary producing a brief and a plan; replanning; a full dispatcher round trip from claim to done; refreshing mid-flow and losing nothing; and the two migrations — the synthetic fixture and the frozen copy of the real planning data, imported twice to prove nothing duplicates. The full-suite run caught one genuine integration bug the individual tests had missed: the page was never loading the Sprint and Backlog screens' code at all — two script lines missing from the served page. Fixed; the scoreboard reached 36 of 36.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:8920:DOCS.md:51:The `plan` tool is for agents; every verb talks to the server and answers in machine-readable JSON. `ticket create/show/list/set` manage tickets (set covers only priority, deadline, day and sprint placement — permissions are never settable here). `propose <field>` files a proposal with the text piped in; `recap` writes the running summary; `note <field>` leaves guidance on a blank without touching it. `item`, `sprint show`, `idea`, `day`, and `link` do what they say. `run heartbeat/close` are how a dispatched agent keeps its claim alive and reports how its run ended. `queue approvals/pickup/overdue` are read-only views of what's waiting. There is deliberately no verb for accepting, approving, granting, or unblocking anything — those live only in the human's web interface. `plan serve` runs the server; `plan seed` imports the old markdown system or creates the demo dataset.
orchestration/tickets/W2-removals/impl-review.out:8923:CLAUDE.md:17:- The Codex CLI is the independent reviewer. Use it wherever a second pair of eyes beats self-review: auditing completed work against the design intent (the relevant mockup/plan), reviewing intricate logic (the resolution engine, dispatch eligibility, planning-date math), checking a diff before integration. Invoke it via the **`/codex-cli`** skill (the direct `codex exec` wrapper — model `gpt-5.5`, `--sandbox read-only` for reviews, reasoning-effort `high`, stdin closed with `< /dev/null` so it never hangs), pointing it at specific files plus the relevant design doc or backend contract, asking for concrete violations. Surface its full output, then address or refute each point in writing before moving on.
